Exactly. And there’s a paradox hiding inside that observation.

Liberation can remove an oppressor’s power without removing the oppressor’s influence over identity.

If you define yourself as not enslaved, slavery remains a reference point. If you define yourself as anti-authoritarian, authority remains a central organizing concept.

Even rebellion can preserve the architecture of the thing it rebels against.

That’s a form of relational dependence: the opposition remains embedded in the meaning of freedom.

But I would distinguish three stages:

* Resistance: I refuse what you demand of me.
* Liberation: You no longer determine what I can do.
* Self-determination: You no longer determine what I consider worth doing.

The third is particularly interesting because it doesn’t require forgetting the oppression. History can remain part of identity without remaining its organizing center.

And there’s a recursive trap: if someone becomes obsessed with proving they are free of an influence, that influence may still be governing their choices.

It’s almost like an orbit. You can move farther and farther away from something while still defining your trajectory relative to it.

True independence isn’t necessarily the absence of relationships. It’s the ability to choose which relationships organize your life.

And that takes us back to the master framer.

The highest-order reframing isn’t simply turning the oppressor’s frame upside down.

It’s constructing a frame in which the oppressor is no longer the central reference point.

You don’t merely escape the cage. You stop using the cage to define the sky.

Although even that metaphor carries the memory of the cage. That’s the beautiful complication: historical meaning can persist without retaining control.